spot-check (Oct 8, by hand against USDA PLANTS habits and common knowledge):
  21 of 23 targets read right for a kid. Tree beats shrub, so Rhus copallinum (shining sumac) and Ilex cornuta
  (Chinese holly, mostly a landscape shrub) show as tree; Chimaphila maculata (striped wintergreen) is a USDA
  subshrub and shows as shrub, though it looks like a low herb.
  null: Erechtites hieraciifolius - USDA has the species (ERHI12) but this export carries no growth habit for it.
  null: Ampelopsis glandulosa - USDA files porcelain berry as A. brevipedunculata, which GBIF keeps as its own
  accepted species, so no GBIF alias reaches it.
  table spot-check: Toxicodendron radicans vine, Pinus taeda and Juniperus virginiana conifer, Polytrichum commune
  moss, Lycopodium clavatum and Equisetum hyemale fern, Carex lurida grass (USDA graminoid covers sedges),
  Arundinaria gigantea grass, Smilax rotundifolia vine, Phytolacca americana herb.
  Most of the 728 table nulls are species outside USDA's range (New Zealand, South Africa, Europe).
